Part VIII.2 -- topological-asymmetry check: do positive and negative pairs
differ in node degree by construction, independent of the model?

positive-pair endpoint degree (in the retained message-passing graph): mean 4.76, median 3
negative-pair endpoint degree: mean 5.80, median 4
Mann-Whitney U p = 1.22e-116

CONFIRMED: positive and negative pairs differ systematically in local graph
degree, independent of anything the model learns -- exactly the topological
asymmetry v1 raised as a plausible (not proven) leak mechanism for H1's
near-ceiling AUPRC. A bilinear scorer over node states can exploit this
degree difference directly. Fixing it requires drawing negatives from nodes
ALSO excluded from message passing (matching topology, not just distance) --
a data-pipeline change, not something this notebook retrains around.
  figure -> results/results_trimodal_1mb_h2signal_fast/figures/25_h1_topological_asymmetry.png
Part IX -- housekeeping tau-median split vs. an external curated gene list

2/20 external housekeeping genes have a computed tau in this gene panel (missing: ['GAPDH', 'TUBB', 'B2M', 'RPLP0', 'YWHAZ', 'SDHA', 'HPRT1', 'TBP', 'PPIA', 'PGK1', 'UBC', 'POLR2A', 'GUSB', 'HMBS', 'PUM1', 'EEF1A1', 'RPS18', 'ALAS1'])

tau for each external housekeeping gene (median split threshold = 0.636):
gene_name
ACTB      0.228
RPL13A    0.238

2/2 (100%) of these externally-curated housekeeping genes fall BELOW the tau-median split, i.e. are correctly classified housekeeping by this project's own method.

A high match rate here is external validation that the tau-median split
captures real housekeeping biology, not an arbitrary statistical artefact
of this dataset. A low match rate would mean the correction from the
unreachable 0.2 default to a median split, while mathematically necessary,
still does not track independently-known housekeeping status well -- report
whichever this run actually shows, not the expected direction.
